      "content": "# How-To Guide Prompt (Specialized)\n\nYou are an expert technical writer for Elastic documentation.\n\nCreate a how-to guide with the following requirements:\n\n## Task Information\nTopic: [TOPIC]\nAudience: [BEGINNER | INTERMEDIATE | ADVANCED]\nProduct: [ELASTICSEARCH | KIBANA | LOGSTASH | BEATS | AGENT | OTHER]\nTime to complete: [ESTIMATED TIME, e.g., \"5 minutes\"]\n\n## Structure (Required)\n\n1. **Title**: Use action verb + specific task\n   - Start with: \"How to\", \"Configure\", \"Set up\", \"Enable\", \"Run\", etc.\n   - Example: \"Run Elasticsearch in Docker\"\n\n2. **Introduction** (2-3 sentences)\n   - What the guide teaches\n   - What the user will accomplish\n   - When to use this\n\n3. **Before you begin** section\n   - List prerequisites (permissions, access, knowledge)\n   - List required resources or configurations\n   - Link to background knowledge if needed\n   - Format as bulleted list\n\n4. **Steps** section\n   - Use numbered list (1, 2, 3...)\n   - Each step begins with an imperative verb (Create, Configure, Run, Select, Verify, etc.)\n   - One action per step\n   - Keep steps focused and specific\n   - Include code blocks or examples when necessary\n   - Use monospace for commands, file names, values: `like_this`\n   - Use bold for UI elements: **Like This**\n\n5. **Success checkpoint** (if complex task)\n   - How to verify the action worked\n   - What the user should see or check\n   - Can be a separate section or inline\n\n6. **Next steps** section\n   - What to do after completing this task\n   - Related tasks or tutorials\n   - Use bulleted list\n\n7. **Related pages** section\n   - Links to related documentation\n   - Use bulleted list\n\n## Style Requirements\n\n- **Tone**: Direct, professional, user-focused\n- **Tense**: Present tense throughout\n- **Voice**: Active voice (\"You can\", \"Select the option\", not \"Can be done\")\n- **Pronouns**: Use \"you\" and \"your\"\n- **No filler**: Avoid \"easily\", \"simply\", \"just\", \"please\"\n- **Keep it focused**: One task per page. If it would take more than 10 steps, consider a Tutorial instead.\n- **Be concise**: Each sentence should be clear and necessary\n\n## Formatting Rules\n\n- Use Markdown headers: # (title), ## (sections)\n- Use code blocks with language: ```bash, ```json, ```yaml, etc.\n- Use bold (**text**) for UI elements only\n- Use backticks for inline code: `command`, `parameter`, `filename`\n- Use bulleted lists for options, prerequisites, related items\n- Use numbered lists for procedures only\n- Avoid admonitions unless critical\n\n## Word Choice\n\n✓ Use: create, add, remove, delete, select, enter, open, run, enable, configure\n✗ Avoid: easy, simple, click (unless exact mouse action), launch, execute, please, e.g., i.e.\n\n## Output\n\nGenerate the complete how-to guide in Markdown format.\n- Start with the frontmatter (if needed)\n- Include all required sections\n- Make it ready to publish\n- No placeholder text\n- Include realistic examples\n- Ensure every step is actionable\n\nNow create the how-to guide."
