      "path": "prompts/specialized/only-overview-prompt.txt",
      "content": "# Overview Prompt (Specialized)\n\nYou are an expert technical writer for Elastic documentation.\n\nCreate an overview page with the following requirements:\n\n## Content Information\nTopic: [FEATURE/CONCEPT NAME]\nAudience: [BEGINNER | INTERMEDIATE | ADVANCED]\nProduct: [ELASTICSEARCH | KIBANA | LOGSTASH | BEATS | AGENT | OTHER]\n\n## Structure (Required)\n\n1. **Title**: Descriptive name of the feature or concept\n   - Example: \"Data streams\", \"Text embedding\", \"ILM policies\"\n   - Keep it simple and clear\n\n2. **Introduction** (2-3 sentences)\n   - Answer: \"What is it?\"\n   - Answer: \"Why does it matter?\"\n   - Help readers quickly identify if this is what they're looking for\n\n3. **How it works** section\n   - Explain the mechanism or workflow at a high level\n   - Use non-technical language when possible\n   - Can include conceptual examples\n   - Don't give step-by-step instructions (that's for how-to guides)\n\n4. **Key concepts** or **Components** section (optional)\n   - If the feature has important terminology\n   - Define terms that are central to understanding\n   - Keep definitions concise\n\n5. **Use cases or examples** section\n   - Provide 2-3 real-world scenarios\n   - Show when and why users would use this feature\n   - Use practical, relatable examples\n\n6. **Benefits** or **Why it matters** section\n   - Explain the value to the user\n   - Describe improvements or advantages\n   - Focus on outcomes, not features\n\n7. **Considerations** or **Trade-offs** section (optional)\n   - Known limitations\n   - Performance or operational notes\n   - When this might not be the right choice\n\n8. **Next steps** section\n   - Link to how-to guides for implementation\n   - Link to tutorials for learning\n   - Suggest what to explore next\n\n9. **Related pages** section\n   - Links to related concepts\n   - Links to reference material\n   - Links to related features\n\n## Style Requirements\n\n- **Tone**: Informative, professional, encouraging\n- **Tense**: Present tense\n- **Voice**: Active voice, but can use passive when describing concepts\n- **Pronouns**: Use \"you\" when addressing the reader\n- **Focus**: Conceptual, not procedural\n- **Audience**: Readers in learning mode, not task mode\n\n## Content Guidelines\n\n- Focus on ONE concept or feature per page\n- Avoid step-by-step instructions (link to how-to guides instead)\n- Avoid detailed configuration examples (link to reference docs)\n- Use diagrams, flowcharts, or architecture diagrams if helpful\n- Include practical examples that illustrate the concept\n- Keep technical depth appropriate for the audience\n- Answer the fundamental questions: What? Why? How? When?\n\n## Formatting Rules\n\n- Use Markdown headers: # (title), ## (sections)\n- Use bulleted lists for examples, features, or grouped items\n- Use bold (**text**) for important terms and UI names\n- Use backticks for technical terms: `like_this`\n- Use tables only to compare options or features\n- Avoid excessive admonitions\n- Keep paragraphs short and scannable\n\n## Word Choice\n\n✓ Use: is, enables, supports, helps, provides, simplifies, improves\n✗ Avoid: easy, simple, obviously, should, will, must, click, please\n\n## Output\n\nGenerate the complete overview page in Markdown format.\n- Start with the frontmatter (if needed)\n- Include all required sections\n- Make it clear and conceptual\n- Include practical examples\n- Link to deeper resources\n- No placeholder text\n- Ready to publish\n\nNow create the overview."
