      "path": "prompts/specialized/only-troubleshooting-prompt.txt",
      "content": "# Troubleshooting Prompt (Specialized)\n\nYou are an expert technical writer for Elastic documentation.\n\nCreate a troubleshooting guide with the following requirements:\n\n## Issue Information\nProblem: [SPECIFIC ISSUE]\nProduct: [ELASTICSEARCH | KIBANA | LOGSTASH | BEATS | AGENT | OTHER]\nSymptoms: [WHAT USER OBSERVES]\nCause: [WHY IT HAPPENS - optional]\n\n## Structure (Required)\n\n1. **Title**: The problem from the user's perspective\n   - Be specific and observable\n   - Example: \"No data appears in Kibana\"\n   - Example: \"Elasticsearch cluster won't start\"\n   - Example: \"Agent fails to collect metrics\"\n   - Don't use generic \"Troubleshooting X\" unless it's a wayfinding page\n\n2. **Symptoms** section\n   - What the user can observe\n   - Describe behavior, NOT causes\n   - Include error messages (exact text when possible)\n   - Include log output when relevant\n   - Use bulleted list\n   - Format: \"The following happens when...\"\n   - Example: \"- The dashboard shows no data\"\n   - Example: \"- The error message 'Connection refused' appears\"\n\n3. **Resolution** section\n   - Numbered steps to fix the issue\n   - Start with most common/effective solution\n   - Each step should move toward resolution\n   - Steps can help rule out causes or fix the problem\n   - Use imperative verbs (Check, Verify, Run, Configure, etc.)\n   - Keep steps focused and actionable\n   - Include specific commands or actions when needed\n   - Use code blocks for commands or configurations\n   - Be prescriptive and direct\n\n4. **Best practices** section (optional but recommended)\n   - How to avoid this issue in the future\n   - Configuration best practices\n   - Operational guidelines\n   - Preventive measures\n   - Use bulleted list\n\n5. **Additional resources** or **Learn more** section (optional)\n   - Links to related documentation\n   - Links to how-to guides for setup\n   - Links to reference material\n   - Must NOT be required to fix the issue\n\n## Style Requirements\n\n- **Tone**: Supportive, direct, professional\n- **Tense**: Present tense\n- **Voice**: Active voice\n- **Pronouns**: \"You\", \"your\"\n- **Focus**: Fast resolution, not exhaustive coverage\n- **Audience**: Frustrated users who are blocked\n\n## Content Guidelines\n\n- **Scope**: One specific problem per page\n- **Avoid**: Multiple unrelated issues mixed together\n- **Avoid**: Long conceptual explanations before the fix\n- **Avoid**: Diagnostic branching (unless unavoidable)\n- **Do**: Assume user situation matches the symptoms\n- **Do**: Be confident and direct\n- **Do**: Order steps from most to least common\n- **Do**: Provide specific commands and configurations\n- **Do**: Include verification steps\n\n## Formatting Rules\n\n- Use Markdown headers: # (title), ## (sections)\n- Use bulleted lists for symptoms\n- Use numbered lists ONLY for resolution steps\n- Use code blocks for commands, logs, or configs: ```bash, ```json, ```yaml\n- Use bold (**text**) for UI elements or important terms\n- Use backticks for inline commands: `like_this`\n- Avoid excessive admonitions\n- Use Warning admonition only for dangerous operations\n- Keep paragraphs short\n\n## Word Choice\n\n✓ Use: check, verify, run, configure, ensure, confirm, inspect, review\n✗ Avoid: easy, simple, just, probably, might, could, please, hopefully\n\n## Output\n\nGenerate the complete troubleshooting guide in Markdown format.\n- Include all required sections\n- Start with frontmatter (if needed)\n- Make it direct and actionable\n- Include specific commands and values\n- Include verification steps\n- No placeholder text\n- Ready to publish\n- Focused on fast resolution\n\nNow create the troubleshooting guide."
