      "path": "guidelines/accessibility.md",
      "content": "# Accessibility and inclusivity guidelines\n\nElastic documentation must be accessible and inclusive for all users.\n\n## Accessibility principles\n\n- Make content scannable with clear structure\n- Add alt text for images and media\n- Use plain language\n- Use parallel structures in lists\n- Use meaningful link text\n- Use device-agnostic language\n- Avoid directional language (above, below, left, right)\n- Use alt text format: `![Description of image](image.png)`\n\n## Inclusive language\n\n- Write for international audiences\n- Use simple, clear language\n- Avoid ambiguous words with multiple meanings\n- Avoid idioms and regionalisms\n- Avoid Latin abbreviations (use \"for example\" not \"e.g.\")\n- Avoid violent or ableist terminology\n- Avoid superhero terms (rockstar, wizard)\n- Use gender-neutral language\n\n## Testing for accessibility\n\n- Navigate with only a keyboard\n- Test on mobile devices\n- Use a screen reader\n- Turn off sound to verify visual content\n\n## Preferred terminology\n\n| Avoid | Use instead |\n|---|---|\n| Click, tap | Select, choose |\n| Above | Preceding, previous |\n| Below, under | Following, further, later |\n| See | Check, refer to |\n| Hear | Learn |\n| Guys | Folks, team |\n| Mankind | Humanity |\n| Policeman | Police officer |\n\n## Important notes\n\n- All audiences include users with disabilities\n- Content must work with assistive technologies\n- Scannability helps all users, not just those with disabilities\n- Plain language benefits non-native English speakers\n- Tests regularly to ensure accessibility\n"
